WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
The premortem and the risk register.

2
00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:10.413
Gary Klein’s premortem, described in the Harvard Business Review in 2007, is a simple exercise with a useful twist.

3
00:00:10.693 --> 00:00:12.635
Instead of asking “what could go wrong?

4
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:17.523
”, you assume the project has already failed and ask each person to write down why.

5
00:00:17.803 --> 00:00:25.675
Klein’s argument is that starting from the failure makes it easier for people with doubts to voice them during planning, when they are still cheap to act on.

6
00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:30.541
For a solo capstone, run it on yourself and, if you can, with a peer or mentor.

7
00:00:30.821 --> 00:00:33.915
Imagine it is week 6 and the defense went badly.

8
00:00:34.195 --> 00:00:34.899
Why?

9
00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:51.776
Typical answers: the labels turned out to be wrong; the corpus license didn’t allow redistribution; the agent worked on dev and collapsed on held-out; costs were ten times the estimate; a reviewer hid an instruction in a retrieved document and the agent followed it;

10
00:00:52.056 --> 00:00:54.275
you ran out of time because the scope grew.

11
00:00:54.555 --> 00:01:11.963
Turn each reason into a row in a risk register: the risk, likelihood and impact (1 to 5 each), exposure (their product), an owner (you, for most), a mitigation, and a trigger that tells you the risk is materializing (“held-out score more than 10 points below dev”).

12
00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:29.309
NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework is a good reference for the wider set of risks AI systems carry, from validity and reliability to security and privacy; you don’t need its full process for a capstone, but skim its categories so your register isn’t only technical.

13
00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:33.365
Rank by exposure and spend week 1 mitigating the top three.

14
00:01:33.645 --> 00:01:44.013
Revisit the register at each milestone, and publish its final state in your write-up: reviewers trust a project more when it shows the risks it looked for and what happened to each.
